before:
This CSS fragment styles HTML with a panel-wrap parent and panel child. Put it in that page's stylesheet.

```css
:root {
  --space: 1.25rem;
  --title-size: clamp(1rem, 4vw, 2.5rem);
}
.panel-wrap {
  container-type: inline-size;
}
.panel {
  padding: var(--space);
}
.panel h2 {
  font-size: var(--title-size);
}
@container (min-width: 540px) {
  .panel {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 2fr;
  }
}
```

The custom values control padding and title size. The wrapper becomes a size query container. Its child's layout changes when that container has enough width, even if the browser window stays the same.

after:
`var()` reads a custom property. This example gives every panel the same padding.
```css
:root {
  --space: 1rem;
}
.panel {
  padding: var(--space);
}
```
